/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A molecule at a solid surface has neighbours on one side only, so the surface attracts molecules from the fluid that touches it.
   - Adsorption releases heat, the heat of adsorption, because held molecules have less energy than free ones.
   - The solute reaches a site in three steps in series: across the stagnant film of fluid around the grain, along the pores into the grain, and onto the surface itself.
   - Syllabi ask for the two types of adsorption side by side.
   - Nearly all separation adsorbers rely on physical adsorption, because the adsorbent must be regenerated thousands of times.
   - Capacity is measured as the loading q, the mass of adsorbate held per unit mass of dry adsorbent, often mg/g or kg/kg.
   - Micropores give most of the area, and their opposite walls overlap their pull, so they hold small molecules strongly.
   - Batch test: W of dry adsorbent shaken with volume V of solution until the concentration stops changing.
   - Polar adsorbents (silica gel, alumina, zeolites) prefer water and other polar molecules; non-polar carbon prefers organic molecules and works in wet streams.
   - Ion-exchange resins look like adsorbents (porous beads in a column) but work by swapping ions of equal charge, as described in Ion exchange principles and equipment.
*/
