/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Flasks with the same volume V of solution but different starting concentrations C0 (or different adsorbent masses W) are shaken at constant temperature until nothing changes.
   - At very low concentration most isotherms are straight, q = KC (Henry's law region).
   - Langmuir assumed a surface of identical sites, each holding one molecule, with no forces between held molecules, so adsorption stops at a single complete layer.
   - qm is the monolayer capacity (mg/g); KL is the affinity constant (L/mg when C is in mg/L); C is the equilibrium concentration.
   - At low C, KLC ≪ 1 and q ≈ qmKLC, the straight Henry's law region.
   - Real surfaces have strong and weak sites.
   - KF is a capacity constant whose units, (mg/g)(L/mg)1/n, depend on n; 1/n (between 0 and 1 for a favourable isotherm) measures how quickly q levels off.
   - The Freundlich isotherm has no plateau: q keeps rising as C rises, so it must not be extrapolated far beyond the data it was fitted to.
   - Pressure.
   - Temperature.
*/
