/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - In one stage, W kg of fresh adsorbent is mixed with V of liquid at C0 and leaves in equilibrium with the final liquid C1.
   - Cross-current: the liquid is treated twice, each time with fresh adsorbent, and the spent adsorbent is filtered off after each stage.
   - Counter-current: fresh adsorbent meets the cleanest liquid in the last stage, then is moved to the first stage to meet the incoming feed, where it leaves loaded almost to q(C0).
   - A moving bed carries the counter-current idea to a column: granules enter at the top, fall slowly as a dense bed against the rising fluid, leave loaded at the bottom and go to a regenerator before returning.
   - Feed at C0 enters a bed of fresh adsorbent.
   - When the front of the MTZ reaches the outlet, the exit concentration C starts to rise.
   - Q is the volumetric feed rate and H the bed height. ts is the stoichiometric time: the time an ideal bed with a vertical front would last.
   - Plants run two or more beds, one adsorbing while another is regenerated by hot gas or steam (temperature swing), lower pressure (pressure swing) or a solvent wash.
*/
