/* book exam traps: The loading at the break point is not C0Qtb/W (3.20 mg/g here): that assumes nothing at all left the bed before tb. Integrate the area above the curve. | An early break point with a slow, spread-out curve means a long LUB and a poorly used bed, not a well-used one. | A favourable isotherm sharpens the front; an unfavourable one spreads it, even with fast mass transfer. */
